\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Where the elimination hypotheses are sharp}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{Two normalized conditional functionals onto nested subalgebras compose to another such functional. That closure does not identify the composite with a third map specified independently. Let #{A=\mathbb R^4} with pointwise product and #{\omega(a,b,c,d)=a}. Set
##{B=\{(a,b,c,c)\},\qquad C=\{(a,a,c,c)\},}
and
##{E_B(a,b,c,d)=(a,b,c,c),\qquad E_C(a,b,c,d)=(a,a,d,d).}
Each map is unital, fixes its subalgebra, pulls constant blocks out of the product, and preserves #{\omega}. For #{u=(0,0,1,0)},
##{E_CE_B(u)=(0,0,1,1),\qquad E_C(u)=(0,0,0,0).}
The state is preserved and the maps are positive under the coordinatewise order. The raw identity #{D=Q\circ P} is a separate hypothesis of \ref{fdip-00KX}.}

\p{A nonzero top coefficient need not be a unit. Over #{\mathbb Z[1/2]}, the weight #{1+3\psi\bar\psi} on one pair has top coefficient #{3}. A normalized functional with that weight would send #{\psi\bar\psi} to #{1/3}, which does not lie in #{\mathbb Z[1/2]}.}

\p{The Wick moment pairing of the [ring-level moment theorem](fdip-008N) can have a radical. Over a field with #{p} elements, #{p} odd, and with one-variable precision #{1}, the odd moments vanish and every even moment of degree at least #{p+1} contains the factor #{p}. Hence #{\omega(x^p f)=0} for every polynomial #{f}, while #{x^p} is nonzero. A faithfulness hypothesis on the moment pairing would exclude this coefficient ring.}

\p{An odd coefficient has a sign. In #{\Lambda(\eta,\xi)}, extracting the coefficient of #{\xi} with retained generators first gives #{I_\xi(\xi)=1} and #{I_\xi(\xi\eta)=-\eta}. Paired extraction avoids this sign because the eliminated top degree is even.}

\p{A positive determinant does not force a positive functional. For one pair, #{K=1}, and the conjugate-linear anti-involution #{\psi^*=\bar\psi}, #{\bar\psi^*=\psi}, the normalized paired state has #{\omega(\psi\bar\psi)=1} and
##{\omega(\psi^*\psi)=\omega(\bar\psi\psi)=-1.}}

\p{The marginal weight has to commute with the subalgebra. On #{M_2(\mathbb C)}, let #{B} be the diagonal matrices and let #{P} be the diagonal projection. For #{w=I+e_{21}}, one has #{P(w)=I}, so #{E(a)=P(aw)} is unital, while
##{E(e_{12}e_{22})=e_{11},\qquad E(e_{12})e_{22}=0.}
The element #{w} does not commute with #{B}.}
